  "Attributes": [
    {
      "Id": 759313,
      "attributeType": "LongString",
      "attributeName": "AIDescription",
      "attributeRelationType": "None",
      "Value": "## 10th Frame (Commodore 64) - Description\n\nStep onto the lanes in *10th Frame*, a realistic and engaging ten-pin bowling simulation for the Commodore 64. Published by U.S. Gold and developed by Access Software in 1986, this sports title delivers a surprisingly deep and skill-based experience.  \n\nPlayers can compete in either open bowling or a full tournament, challenging themselves against the computer or up to seven other players. The game boasts a unique 3D perspective, viewed from behind the bowler, offering a compelling visual representation of the action. Mastering the gameplay requires precision and timing; players control the bowler's position and aim using a target cursor, then utilize a power meter to determine shot speed and spin.  \n\nThree difficulty levels – Kids, Amateur, and Professional – cater to players of all skill levels.  Printable scorecards add a touch of authenticity to your bowling prowess.  Praised by critics for its slick programming and realistic simulation, *10th Frame* is a classic sports game that remains enjoyable even today."
    },
